.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 201 PM PDT Fri Oct 2 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

Satellite imagery this afternoon shows high clouds streaming over
head and below that most of the stratus from this morning seems to
have eroded. Webcams around Half Moon and San Francisco Bay show a
few lingering low and mid level clouds. The marine layer this
morning was around 500-800ft per the OAK sounding, which was on
track with some of the hi-res models. Expect the marine layer to
continue to compress leaving us at 500ft for tonight, which would
limit stratus development. If it re-develops, it`d start around San
Francisco`s and expand south. The highest confidence for stratus re-
development is around the Monterey Bay and the along the Central
Coast with the potential for patches of dense fog. Any fog or
stratus that develops should be short lived, leading to a warm and
sunny Saturday.

Saturday kicks off the warming trend for the upcoming week, with a
Heat Advisory going into effect at 11AM Saturday and running through
10PM Monday for the majority of the inland areas. Widespread
Moderate HeatRisk with pockets of Major HeatRisk forecast are during
this time. Temperatures might be at risk on Saturday if stratus
doesn`t develop, leading to warmer temperatures near the coast. This
will be something to monitor tonight and into tomorrow morning.
&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 201 PM PDT Fri Oct 2 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

As noted above, a Heat Advisory will be in effect from 11AM Saturday
and running through 10PM Monday. Models, including the NBM, have
trended with warmer temperatures into early to mid next week even
though an upper level shortwave trough tries to move through Monday.
Given the trends, opted to expand the Heat Advisory into Monday, but
held off on any decisions for Tuesday or Wednesday. Upper level
ridging builds back in Tuesday through Thursday, with a potential
pattern change occuring Friday or Saturday. Temperatures should
start ease Thursday and with the trend continuing into Friday.

With all that being said, no records are in jeopardy as October is
one of the hottest months for the region.

